"Look inside a phone or a computer and you will see small black squares on a green board. Those squares are not the chips themselves. Each one is a package: a tough case with a chip inside. The package protects the thin silicon from bumps, dust, and damp. It also connects the chip's tiny contacts to the much bigger world of the circuit board.": "48baf3b7ea59",
"Look inside a phone or a computer and you will see small black squares on a green board. Those squares are not the chips themselves. Each one is a package: a tough case with a chip inside. The package protects the thin silicon from bumps, dust, and damp. It also connects the chip's tiny contacts to the much bigger world of the circuit board. For a long time, that was the whole job. One chip went into one package, and the packages were soldered onto a board a few centimeters apart. But chips kept getting faster, and the trip between them began to matter. Data from the logic chip, which does the thinking, had to cross the board to reach the memory chip, which holds the data. Every trip took time and energy. Why not just make one giant chip with everything on it? Because chips cannot grow without limit. The machine that prints the circuits can only expose an area of about 26 by 33 millimeters at a time. Bigger chips also mean fewer chips on each wafer, and one speck of dust can ruin a bigger piece. So engineers took a different road: keep the chips small, and bring them very close together. The first way is to place bare chips side by side on a thin slice of silicon called an interposer. Its wires are far finer than the wires on a circuit board, so the chips can sit almost touching. This is called 2.5D packaging. Designers can mix and match small chips, called chiplets, a little like building with LEGO bricks. The second way is to stack chips on top of one another, like floors in a building. Tiny copper posts called through-silicon vias, or TSVs, run straight through the chips like elevators. Now the data travels up a few floors instead of across a city. Shorter wires mean faster signals and less wasted power. But a stack traps heat, so engineers must plan carefully how to keep it cool. After packaging, every chip is tested before it leaves the factory. Packaging and testing together are a big industry in Taiwan. ASE, founded in Kaohsiung in 1984, is the world's largest company that packages and tests chips for other companies. So the next time you hear about “advanced packaging,” think of a tall building: many chips, living close together, working as one.": "193d95571e3e",
